  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p><strong>Mount Daniel</strong> rises to 7,963 feet in Washington. With roughly 3,520 feet of topographic prominence, it stands well above the surrounding terrain. The Mount Daniel Trail covers 3.4 miles with about 2,267 feet of elevation gain \u2014 a steady climb. It's rated Hard overall.</p>\n  <h3>The Route</h3><p>Most of the climbing comes early \u2014 the first half of the route does the bulk of the work before the grade eases. The steepest stretch arrives around mile 0, gaining roughly 1,600 feet per mile \u2014 expect a slow, deliberate pace through here. From the trailhead at about 3,097 feet, the route tops out near 5,364 feet.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=47.50462,-121.17157\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">47.50462, -121.17157</a> (tap for driving directions). It's roughly 55 miles east of Seattle, Washington. Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>Late May through October is the main season. Snow and ice linger into spring and return early in fall at this latitude and elevation; in winter this becomes a different, more serious undertaking requiring traction devices and warm layers.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>The Hard rating comes from the National Park Service difficulty formula, which weighs total distance against elevation gain. Most hikers should budget about 2.5 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Mount Stuart, Disappointment Peak and Seven Fingered Jack \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
